      "speech": "On top, a bichon and a corgi racing down a sidewalk. The dots and lines are point tracks, pink for the bichon's face, red for the corgi's. The edit: slow the corgi down to flip the race, so I pull its red track back. The bottom row is the output. Same dogs, same street, new motion. That's MotionV2V."
    },
    {
      "title": "Why not GWTF?",
      "elements": [
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_motionv2v/race_dimmed.png",
          "rect": [
            40,
            140,
            880,
            214
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "box",
          "rect": [
            36,
            136,
            302,
            222
          ],
          "color": "#C00000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Anything not in frame 1 gets made up",
          "rect": [
            40,
            390,
            880,
            50
          ],
          "size": 28,
          "color": "#C00000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "ATI, GWTF: first frame in, rest generated",
          "rect": [
            40,
            446,
            880,
            32
          ],
          "size": 22,
          "color": "#000000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "So why can't GWTF do this? ATI and my own GWTF are image-to-video. It takes this first frame and generates the rest. Anything that's not in frame one, like a car that drives in later, gets made up. ReVideo keeps the original outside an edit mask, and breaks when the camera moves. And the classic editing trick, invert the video back to noise and regenerate it, needs the output to line up with the input frame by frame. Change the motion, and that breaks."
    },
    {
